031,"fs":0.01279,"n":4,"k":1,"t":"To have a limit L as x approaches c, a function ƒ must be defined on both sides of c and its values ƒ(x) must approach L as x approaches c from either side. That is, ƒ must be defined in some open interval about c, but not necessarily at c. Because of this, ordinary limits are called two-sided."}]}